 it. Config loads and committer lanes fetch into it, and every committer and executor worktree is cut from it (lanes under `~/.ard/work/<owner>/<repo>/lanes/`). `ard serve` never writes to the operator's checkout: it reads it only for the `origin` fallback of `--repo` and the local `paused` key. To rebuild a damaged clone, stop serve, `rm -rf` it and restart. Upgrading from a version that used the checkout: ard deletes its old lane and job worktree directories without running git there, so the old `refs/ard/*` refs and the dangling worktree registrations stay in the checkout until you remove them (`git for-each-ref --format='delete %(refname)' refs/ard/ | git update-ref --stdin; git worktree prune`). Every `git worktree add`, `remove` and `prune` on the controller's clone, by the executor and the committer's lanes alike, takes one in-process lock keyed by the clone's common git directory, because git's worktree bookkeeping is not safe under concurrent admin commands; fetches, commits and pushes do not take it. Each such command also has a 30 s deadline, and cleanup runs even when the job's context is cancelled, so one hung git cannot stall every lane. Do not run `git worktree prune` (or add/remove) in `~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git` by hand while `ard serve` runs: the lock is per process and cannot cover a human's git. The operator's own checkout is free to use. The host keeps two files per job: `<base>.log` (stdout and stderr combined, the heartbeat source and the committed log) and `<base>.stdout` (stdout only, parsed by the adapter). A read-only job (`ReadOnly`, used by scanners) gets the worktree mounted `:ro` under Docker, or `chmod -R a-w` on the host. The heartbeat is the log file's mtime while the PID is alive; liveness alone is not a beat. Phase 0 local jobs do not survive a controller restart: the pipes belong to the controller, so after a restart the job reports `orphaned` (PID alive, start time verified) or `gone`, and the Reaper kills and fails it. The executor is not an effect: it never calls GitHub or commits, so jobs run normally under `--dry-run`. Sidecar auth: the sidecar fetch runs in the controller with the GitHub App installation token in a host-scoped `http.<url>.extraheader` passed through git's environment, never argv; the job receives plain files and no credential, and the cache stores no remote and no token. The App must be installed on the sidecar repo with contents:read. Host-mode jobs can reach `~/.ard/sidecars`, but it holds no credential. Host-mode jobs run as the controller's uid: they can read the GitHub App key and `~/.config/gh` by absolute path, write the controller's clone, and undo `chmod a-w`. Host mode is allowed for every role ([#84](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/84)), but a role that reads untrusted input (every triage, worker and reviewer role, and an LLM scanner) warns loudly when it has no `Dockerfile`; deterministic scanners are exempt, and nothing warns under `executor: k8s`, where every job runs in a container. `ard config check` and the first successful config load of `ard serve` (and any role a later config adds) log one `executor.host_untrusted` WARN per such role and also write it to stderr, so it survives `--log-level`. The local executor logs the WARN again for every host-mode job of such a role and appends a `host_untrusted` row to the `events` table. Each warning lists what the job can reach, including `~/.ard` (state, config snapshots, the pause file, other jobs' worktrees and logs). The owner accepts this risk while ard runs only on owner-controlled repos, and revisits it when moving to the spark cluster, where every job runs in a container once the k8s executor (#54) lands. Until then the Gate 1 dry-run soak on spark (see Rollout and Delivery) runs `ard serve` with the local executor in a pod, and the deterministic cve scanner runs there as a host subprocess of the controller container: its role directory has no `Dockerfile`, and the `ard` image carries its pinned tools. Only deterministic scanners run in that pod.\ndocs/proposal.md:269:**Agent CLIs plug in through adapters.** Each adapter turns (prompt file, context paths, budget) into a command line and parses the exit code and, where the CLI reports them, turns and cost. For Claude Code that is `claude -p --output-format=stream-json --verbose --restricted --safe-mode --strict-mcp-config --disable-slash-commands --permission-mode=dontAsk --settings='{\"includeGitInstructions\":false,\"disableAllHooks\":true}' --tools=… --allowed-tools=… --disallowed-tools=… --model <model> --max-budget-usd <max_budget_usd> --append-system-prompt-file <role prompt> -- <message>` (`--verbose` is required by `stream-json` in print mode). Every stage (scanner, triage, worker, review) gets the same tool profile: `--tools` and `--allowed-tools` are `Read,Glob,Grep,Edit,Write,Bash`, and `--disallowed-tools` is `Bash(git commit *),Bash(git push *),Bash(git remote *),Bash(gh *)` (decided, #79). ard does not pass `--bare`: it limits the CLI to Bash, Read and Edit, and `--tools` cannot widen it (#105). The planted-config isolation `--bare` gave comes instead from `--restricted` (only managed settings and `--settings` load), `--safe-mode` (no CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md, skills, agents, hooks, MCP servers or auto memory), `--strict-mcp-config`, `--disable-slash-commands`, and `--settings` that turns off hooks and the git status snapshot (commit subjects are third-party text). `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_BACKGROUND_TASKS=1` is set as a fixed env var (the executor allows it) so Bash cannot detach commands. The integration tests pin each of these against a worktree with planted config and must be re-run on every `TestedVersion` bump. The tool list is not a security boundary, and the deny list is advisory, because shell indirection gets around it. The real boundaries are the missing GitHub token (Docker; see the host-mode note above, #84), the committer, which commits only `.ard/<issue#>/` from a triage or review job (other edits are discarded with the worktree), and the diff guard on worker changes. An explicit tool list from the caller (`Spec.Tools`) replaces the allow list and keeps the deny list. The user message holds only fixed text and the paths of the untrusted input files. The installed CLI accepts an undocumented `--max-turns`; ard does not rely on it, so a per-job dollar cap and the executor's hard timeout bound each run. Agents run with an empty per-job CLI config dir and HOME and receive exactly one model credential, passed by name by the executor and never visible to the adapter: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) or a subscription OAuth token (`CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN`, from `claude setup-token`); that rests on the environment, not on a CLI flag. `ard serve` chooses the credential from its own environment: `ARD_AGENT_AUTH` is `auto` (the default; the token if set, else the key), `oauth` or `apikey`, and an explicit mode never falls back to the other variable. Only one is ever passed, because the CLI prefers the API key and would silently bill it (pinned by an integration test). A missing credential does not stop startup, because deterministic scanners need none: `ard serve` logs one INFO line, and a job that needs one fails with a clear error. The executor creates the per-job config dir as `<job home>/.claude` (0700) in `prepare` (`Handle.View.ConfigDir`; under Docker it is a bind mount at `/ard-config`), refuses to start a job whose config dir is not that empty directory, and removes it with the job home; the Reaper's `KeepWorktree` retention keeps it too, and it holds no credential, since the credential travels only in the environment. `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBPROCESS_ENV_SCRUB=1`, which would keep the token out of Bash children, is not set (decided, #125). Residual risk: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) is inherited by Bash children; on the tested release the CLI does not pass `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` to them; and either credential can still be read from the `claude` process's own environment (`/proc/$PPID/environ` on Linux, `ps eww` on macOS). The container is the boundary: it holds no GitHub token, and the credential is scoped to the model. The scrub is off because on 2.1.328 it requires bubblewrap (and socat) plus unprivileged user namespaces; Docker blocks those under ard's flags (`--cap-drop ALL`, `no-new-privileges`, default seccomp), so every Bash call would fail closed and without bwrap the CLI refuses to start. On macOS host mode it worked and matched the `dontAsk` outcomes. It can be revisited for host mode or a loosened container. What agent Bash can read differs by mode: under Docker, only the job's own credential; in host mode, far more, because any same-uid process's environment is readable (`ps eww <pid>` on macOS, `/proc/<pid>/environ` on Linux), including `ard serve`'s own, which holds both credentials. The scrub would not close that; it is part of the host-mode risk of #84. `kimi` and `dsh-tui` both run headless with `-p`; neither has a turn cap and `kimi` reports no cost, so turns and cost are optional in the adapter result. `config.yaml` names the default CLI and any per-role override, so a role can move between CLIs without touching its prompt. The Claude adapter also reads a usage limit and token usage from the stream: a limit comes from a `rate_limit_event` with status `rejected` (its `resetsAt` is the reset time), from the CLI's own error text (`You've hit your session, weekly, Opus or Sonnet limit`) and from `rate_limit` or `billing_error` errors, and it records `usage` and `modelUsage` token counts (by input, output, cache write and cache read; per model when the result has `modelUsage`, which is preferred for accounting). That text is matched only on paths the CLI itself writes (an assistant message carrying an `error`, or an errored result with HTTP 429 or `api_error`), never in agent output, because an agent can be made to print the phrase. With an API key a 429 stays a transient `rate_limit` retry; only subscription (OAuth) runs have usage limits. The committed fixtures are scrubbed real captures where a human has made them (`testdata/CAPTURE.md`) and clearly named `synthetic_*` files elsewhere.\ndocs/proposal.md:450:- **Release.** An annotated tag `vX.Y.Z` (`git tag -a`) builds that commit with `VERSION=vX.Y.Z`, pushes `:vX.Y.Z` and moves `:latest` when it is the highest version. Release tags are annotated because Jenkins builds only tags at most a day old and dates a lightweight tag by its commit, so a lightweight tag on an older commit never builds. Releases do not change what is deployed, which follows `main`.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01HGat59QC89kjCLXQwMz3UK","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
